$\left[2{+}1\right]$ Cycloisomerization of Enynes. In order to demonstrate the enhanced Lewis acid catalytic activity of the synthesized zwitterionic MOFs, we first examined their reactivity toward the cycloisomerization of enynes.17 Cationic $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}.$ -porphyrin is known to catalyze the cycloisomerization of 1,6-enynes where the coordination capability of the counter anion is essential toward the chemoselectity.18 In general, weakly coordinated anions seem to promote the rare cyclopropane-annulated endocyclized six-membered product via an intramolecular stepwise $\left[2{+}1\right]$ cycloaddition, with the highest yield of $75\%$ (with TFPB, tetrakis[3,5-bis(trifluoromethyl)-phenyl]- borate).18 To our delight, employing only 1 $1\mathrm{mol\%}$ of $\mathrm{UNLPF–14–Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}$ as the catalyst, an excellent yield and selectivity toward the endo-product 5a $(95\%$ isolated yield) was obtained within only 8h (Table 1, entry 1). In contrast, $\mathrm{PCN-223\mathrm{-}M n}^{\mathrm{III}}[\mathrm{SbF_{6}}]$ exhibits essentially the same catalytic activity and selectivity for the endoproduct 5a $(63\%/31\%$ yield of endo/exo product) as the homogenous catalyst $\mathrm{MnTPP[SbF_{6}]}$ (entries $^4$ and 7), likely due to the presence of the extra-framework $S{\mathrm{bF}_{6}}^{-}$ . As expected, the presence of the strongly coordinating chloride anion in UNLPF- ${\bf\sigma}_{13}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{Cl}$ completely inhibits the catalytic activity (entry 2), consistent with PCN-223- $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ (entry 3) and the homogeneous catalyst MnTPPCl
1 ${\mathrm{TPP}}=$ tetraphenylporphyrin) (entry 5). This result highlights the essential role of the complete charge separation that is only attainable in our zwitterionic MOFs. Furthermore, UNLPF- $\mathbf{\cdot14^{-Mn}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ exhibits an excellent recyclability: a $92\%$ yield was obtained after three times of catalyst reuse (entry 8), while still maintaining good crystallinity (Figure S8). Additionally, ICP-MS performed on the supernatant of the reaction mixture indicated a low catalyst leaching of $0.20~\mathrm{mol\%}$ (Scheme $S_{3}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ).
To demonstrate the scope of the cycloisomerization of 1,6-enynes using UNLPF- $\cdot\mathbf{\bar{14}}\mathbf{-Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}$ as the catalyst we carried out the reaction using different enynes 4 (Table 2) under the optimized reaction condition (Table 1, entry 1). The reaction shows good tolerance toward terminal, alkyl- and dialkyl-substituted, as well as phenyl-substituted alkenes (4a-4d). Interestingly, we did not observe the decomposition of substrate 4d in our catalytic system, as was the case in the presence of MnTPP[TFPB].18 Our MOF-based catalyst also exhibits excellent to good reactivity toward 1,6-enynes that contain internal alkynes such as 4e $88\%$ yield of 5e) and 4f $(67\%$ yield of $\mathbf{5^{f}})$ , for which the cationic $\mathrm{MnTPP[SbF_{6}]}$ catalyst exhibited negligible catalytic activity (Table 2). According to the proposed mechanism18 (Scheme S4), the coordination of the Lewis acid to the $\beta{\cdot}C$ of the alkyne with respect to the nitrogen tether is the first and likely the rate limiting step leading to the endo-product.18 However, in the presence of a weakly coordinating anion (e.g. OTf–) the catalysts’ active site is likely sterically hindered, in which case the $\gamma$ - C of the alkyne may coordinate instead further leading to the exo-product (Table 1, entry 6). Thus, the high selectivity of UNLPF- $\mathbf{\cdot14^{-Mn}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ towards the endo-product is likely due to the “non-coordinating” nature of the SBU-based counter anion. In effect this renders the Lewis acidic metal center more “naked”, allowing for substrates with sterically demanding internal alkynes (4e and 4f) to coordinate and further undergo the $\left[2{+}1\right]$ cycloaddition. To the best of our knowledge, transition-metal (Rh, Ir, Pt, and Au) complexes are usually required to prepare endo cyclic products 5e or 5f with however poor to moderate yields.19
