We next examined the UNLPF- $\mathbf{\cdot14^{-Mn}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ catalyzed $[3+2]$ cycloaddition of aziridine 7 with different alkenes 8. Styrenes with electron-withdrawing substituents such as chloride (8a) and bromide (8c) as well as electrondonating substituents such as methoxy (8d) and tertbutyl (8e) all gave rise to the pyrrolidine product 9 in excellent yields $(>88\%$ , Table 3). Remarkably, the internal alkene, anethole $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{8h})$ , is also a suitable substrate for aziridine 7a to give pyrrolidine 9h $(87\%$ yield, 12h). In contrast, $\mathrm{MnTPP[SbF_{6}]}$ only afforded a low yield $(19\%$ , 24h), in which the major by-product was determined to be the Friedel-Craft arylation product.22 We attribute this result to capability of UNLPF- ${\bf\cdot14^{-}}{\bf M}{\bf n}^{\mathrm{III}}$ to better stabilize the $^{1,3}$ -dipole intermediate following the C-N cleavage of the aziridine, further allowing for more challenging styrene substrates to attack (Scheme $S{5}\mathrm{\overline{{{\Omega}}}}$ ). To the best of our knowledge, this is the first reported example of a pyrrolidine formed from an aromatic internal alkene and an aziridine. Our catalyst protocol may be used to prepare the derivatives of pharmaceutically relevant compounds such as Atrasentan (Table $_3$ , inset). Moreover, aziridines with electron-withdrawing substituting groups such as fluoride $\left(\mathbf{76}\right)$ , chloride $\mathbf{\rho}(\mathbf{78})$ , and trifluromethyl (7f) were also suitable substrates, and the functionalized pyrrolidines were obtained in excellent yields $(>88\%)$ .
