$[4^{+2}]$ Hetero-Diels-Alder Cycloaddition of Aldehydes with Dienes. To extend our zwitterionic MOF catalysis protocol to other cationic metalloporphyrin systems, we tested the catalytic activity of UNLPF-16-FeIII towards the $[4^{+2}]$ hetero-Diels-Alder cycloaddition of aldehydes with dienes.23 This reaction can be used to prepare pyrans, a class of synthetic targets for biologically relevant compounds.24 It was recently reported that cationic $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -porphyrin can promote the chemoselective cycloaddition of unactivated, electron deficient aldehydes with simple, poorly reactive dienes.25 Similarly, the coordination strength of the counter anion dictates the catalytic activity in that the WCA, ${\mathrm{SbF}}_{6}^{\mathrm{~-~}}$ or $\mathrm{BF}_{4}^{-}$ , was necessary for a high catalytic activity.25 Indeed, the reaction of benzaldehyde (10a) with $^{2,3}$ -dimethyl- $^{\cdot_{1,3}}$ -butadiene $\mathbf{\Pi}(\mathbf{u})$ in the presence of zwitterionic UNLPF-16-FeIII in toluene at $80^{\circ}C$ for 8h afforded pyran 12a in $96\%$ yield (Table $^4$ and Table S8), faster than $\mathrm{FeTPP[BF_{4}]}$ $(5~\mathrm{mol\%}$ loading, $92\%$ in 12h).25 Again a low catalyst leaching of $0.06~\mathrm{mol}\%$ was demonstrated by ICP-MS for the supernatant of the reaction mixture (Scheme S8). As expected, in the presence of UNLPF- $^{15}$ -FeIIICl or $\mathrm{PCN-}223\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{\hat{I}I I}}\mathrm{Cl}$ the reaction became retarded (Table S8) due to the presence of the strongly coordinating $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ , similar to the result from FeTPPCl.25
